WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.900
Retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, helps an AI system answer from source material instead of relying only on the model's trained knowledge.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:18.280
That source material may be documents, tickets, messages, wiki pages, contracts, support cases, or records from internal systems.

3
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:25.580
The value is clear: users can ask natural language questions and receive answers grounded in organizational knowledge.

4
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.980
The permission challenge is just as important.

5
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.560
A user may be allowed to ask a question, but not allowed to see every document that could answer it.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:39.820
If retrieval ignores permissions, the model can receive content the user should never have accessed.

7
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.920
The model is not always the original source of the exposure.

8
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:54.900
The exposure can come from ingestion, metadata loss, index design, retrieval filtering, prompt construction, logs, caches, or output formatting.

9
00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:01.340
Permission-aware RAG treats retrieved content as evidence, not unrestricted authority.

10
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:10.480
The system should preserve access boundaries from the source repository through ingestion, chunking, indexing, retrieval, generation, and final response.

11
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.380
It should also preserve enough context for operations teams to audit what happened later.

12
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.540
This course focuses on defensive design.

13
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:28.080
The goal is to build RAG systems where identity, metadata, indexing, retrieval, generation, logging, testing, and governance work together.

14
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.260
A safe system is not created by a prompt alone.

15
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.960
It is created by a full pipeline that respects permissions at every stage.
